  {"id": "F1", "category": "quantifier", "severity": "high", "decl": "triangleRamseyNumber_log_sharp_coefficients", "line": 37, "paper_ref": "Theorem 1.1 (sharp exponents)", "explanation": "The statement is `∃ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → …`. Taking ε = 0 makes the hypothesis false, so the theorem is trivially true and says nothing. The intended statement is `∀ ε > 0, ∀ᶠ k in atTop, …`, i.e. the 1/3 − o(1) and 1 + o(1) exponents. As written, the sharp coefficients are not formalized at all.", "polarity": "lean weaker", "probe_idea": "Prove `∃ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → False` via ⟨0, lt_irrefl 0⟩ to show vacuity. Restate as `∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ∀ᶠ k : ℕ in atTop, ((1/3:ℝ) - ε) * k * Real.log k ≤ Real.log (triangleRamseyNumber k) ∧ Real.log (triangleRamseyNumber k) ≤ (1 + ε) * k * Real.log k`."},
  {"id": "F2", "category": "type-default", "severity": "low", "decl": "triangleRamseyNumber", "line": 15, "paper_ref": null, "explanation": "For k = 0, labelings of K_n with n ≥ 2 do not exist, so the defined value is 2. This is a harmless edge case, since every claim is either asymptotic or restricted to k ≥ 2.", "polarity": "n/a", "probe_idea": "`triangleRamseyNumber 0 = 2`"},
